  "subtype": "causal_attribution",
  "outcome_variable": "departmental_declined_rate_variation (expense decline / rejection)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "asset_type",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Type of asset (hardware/software/cloud/travel) plausibly drives whether an expense is declined; a candidate cause of the decline outcome. Also varies by department, but its primary role here is a spend-category treatment whose effect on decline can be attributed."
    },
    {
      "name": "entry_origin",
      "causal_role": "treatment",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Manual vs auto-generated entry plausibly affects decline likelihood (manual entries more error-prone), a candidate cause of the outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "has_source_id",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Presence of a source_id (linkage/provenance) plausibly affects both how an expense is entered/originated and whether it is declined; a common cause of process treatment and outcome."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_lag",
      "causal_role": "collider",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Processing lag is downstream of both the entry process and the decline decision (declined items may not be processed; 'not_processed' overlaps with decline). Conditioning on it induces bias; it is an outcome-related effect, not a common cause."
    },
    {
      "name": "vendor_brand",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Vendor/brand plausibly influences both asset_type (treatment) and decline likelihood (approved-vendor policies), a common cause surfaced from the CI/description data."
    },
    {
      "name": "department_function_type",
      "causal_role": "confounder",
      "named_in_query": true,
      "rationale": "A grouping/attribute of department, which is the explicit axis of variation named in the query. Plausibly causes both the type of spend (treatment) and the decline rate, but it is essentially a re-encoding of the department already named in the query."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_travel_related",
      "causal_role": "mediator",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Largely a derivative of asset_type/spend category (travel_equipment). It sits on the path between asset_type treatment and decline rather than being a common cause; redundant/mediating."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_new_asset_acquisition",
      "causal_role": "other",
      "named_in_query": false,
      "rationale": "Constant (true for all 500 rows). No variance means it can identify nothing; contributes no causal information."
    }
  ],
  "treatment_present": true,
  "treatment_columns": ["asset_type", "entry_origin"],
  "confounders": [
    {"name": "has_source_id", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.45},
    {"name": "vendor_brand", "named_in_query": false, "plausibility": 0.5},
    {"name": "department_function_type", "named_in_query": true, "plausibility": 0.5}
  ],
  "confounder_quality": 0.4,
  "rationale": "A treatment is present (asset_type/entry_origin as candidate causes of decline). Some genuinely surfaced confounders exist (vendor_brand, has_source_id) that plausibly cause both the spend/process treatment and the decline outcome and are NOT named in the query. However, the one confounder most directly tied to the query axis (department_function_type) is a re-encoding of 'department', already named. Several added columns are problematic: processing_lag is a collider tangled with the outcome, is_travel_related is a mediator/redundant with asset_type, and is_new_asset_acquisition is constant and uninformative. So the set only partially realises a proper causal-adjustment design, with moderate-to-weak confounder quality."
}
